2. Lossless compression, stated precisely
In Shannon's information theory (1948): a source with entropy H(X) can be compressed to a minimum of H(X) bits per symbol, and no further. Compression below H(X) is necessarily lossy — information is destroyed. Compression to exactly H(X) is lossless — the original can be perfectly reconstructed. The Shannon limit:

L_min = H(X) = -Σ p(x) log p(x)

Lossless compression removes redundancy (the gap between the actual encoding and the entropy limit). It does not remove information; it removes the slack in the encoding.

Key facts:

Lossless compression is bounded: no compression can preserve all information below the Shannon limit.
The Shannon limit is a property of the source, not the encoder: a source with high entropy (e.g., random data) cannot be compressed at all; a source with low entropy (e.g., English text) can be compressed ~5x.
Information is conserved in lossless compression: the input and output have the same information content; only the representation differs.
The deep fact: lossless compression is the operation that preserves information while removing redundancy. It is, structurally, what attention does — preserve the salient while damping the redundant.

3. Attention — the universal selective operation
The attention mechanism (Bahdanau et al. 2014, Vaswani et al. 2017) computes:

Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QK^T / √d_k) V

where Q (query), K (key), V (value) are learned projections of the input. The softmax creates a probability distribution over the keys; the value vectors are weighted by this distribution and summed. The output is a weighted average where the weights are determined by query-key similarity.

Structural facts:

Attention is selective compression: it produces a fixed-size output (a weighted average) from a variable-size input. Information is preserved in the weighted sum, but compressed into a single vector.
Attention is differentiable concentration: the softmax temperature controls how "peaked" the attention is. Low temperature → uniform attention (no compression); high temperature → one-pointed attention (maximal compression).
Attention is a form of lossless compression: with sufficient capacity, the attention mechanism can reconstruct any element of the input — the compression is lossy only when capacity is constrained.
The deep fact: attention is the mathematical structure of "what to attend to" — the universal selective operation that appears whenever a system must process more information than it can hold.

4. Physics as an attention mechanism — the Feynman path integral
This is the connection that puts physics in the same frame. Feynman's path integral (1948) computes the amplitude for a process by summing over all possible paths:

K(x_b, t_b; x_a, t_a) = ∫ D[x(t)] e^(iS[x(t)]/ℏ)

where S[x(t)] is the action of the path x(t). In the classical limit (ℏ → 0), the integral is dominated by the stationary-action path — the path for which δS = 0. All other paths interfere destructively and cancel; only the stationary path "survives."

Structural reading: the path integral is an attention mechanism. It sums over all paths (all input features), weights them by e^(iS/ℏ) (the attention weights), and the stationary-action path is the "attended" path that survives. The principle of least action is the universe's selective attention — the universe attends to the stationary path and ignores the rest.

In quantum mechanics (ℏ ≠ 0), the attention is not perfectly concentrated — many paths contribute, with the stationary path weighted highest. As ℏ → 0 (classical limit), attention concentrates. In the deep quantum regime, attention is diffuse.

This is the structural isomorphism: physics is a concentration mechanism on action. The principle of least action is the universe's attention — the same operation that appears in transformers and in contemplative concentration.

