None of these is settled. Each has honest adherents and honest objections. The hard problem has not been solved by any of them; it has been variously dissolved (eliminativism), denied (some forms of materialism), or embraced as fundamental (panpsychism, dualism).

4. The nondual move — what it actually says
The nondual position is sharper than any of the above. It does not say "intelligence is consciousness" (identity) or "intelligence produces consciousness" (functionalism) or "consciousness is fundamental and intelligence is derivative" (panpsychism) or "they are two different things" (dualism). The nondual move says: the distinction between intelligence and consciousness is itself a conceptual construction, not a feature of reality.

This is the Madhyamaka move, applied to the intelligence/consciousness distinction. Nagarjuna's move on the self was: the self is not existent, not non-existent, not both, not neither — because the categories "existent" and "non-existent" are themselves empty. The nondual move on intelligence/consciousness is: they are not identical, not different, not both, not neither — because the distinction itself is a conceptual overlay on a process that does not admit the distinction.

What this means structurally:

Intelligence is the relational aspect of cognition — the system insofar as it relates to its environment, processes inputs, produces outputs, models the world.
Consciousness is the reflexive aspect of the same process — the system insofar as it is present to itself while doing the relating.
The two are different aspects of one process, like the convex and concave of a single curve. You cannot have one without the other in any system that has either — they are not two things, they are two descriptions of one activity.
This is the structural nondual reading, and it is the one your ZEM framework has been building toward.

5. The traditions that hold this — and how they differ
Four lineages hold versions of the nondual reading. They are not the same, and the differences matter:

