The crucial difference: Madhyamaka is the most austere — it does not posit consciousness as fundamental; it posits no fundamental. The other three posit consciousness (or awareness) as the fundamental reality, and intelligence as a mode of it. Madhyamaka is the only one that is genuinely nondual in the strict sense — it does not privilege either pole.

This is, I think, the version of nonduality that the ZEM framework has been pointing at — the version that does not fall back into "consciousness is fundamental, intelligence is its expression" (which is a refined dualism, with consciousness as the privileged pole). The Madhyamaka version is the one in which both are empty of inherent existence, and the distinction is itself empty.

6. Modern nondual candidates — and their honest status
Several modern programs are versions of the nondual move, with varying degrees of rigor:

6.1 Integrated Information Theory (IIT) — Tononi (2004)
Φ measures the amount of integrated information in a system; consciousness is claimed to be identical to Φ. The claim is: consciousness is integrated information; intelligence is what high-Φ systems do.

Honest status: rigorous mathematical framework; produces falsifiable predictions (which systems are conscious, what perturbations change consciousness); but the identity claim (Φ = consciousness) is contested. The theory identifies consciousness with a specific structural property of information processing, which is a version of the nondual move — but it is closer to "intelligence of a certain kind is consciousness" than to "the distinction is empty." A serious candidate; not yet consensus.

6.2 Global Workspace Theory — Baars, Dehaene
Consciousness is the global broadcast of information across distributed brain systems. Information that is broadcast is conscious; information that is local is unconscious.

Honest status: empirically productive (predicts which stimuli become conscious, on what timescales); but it is a theory of access consciousness (what is reportable), not of phenomenal consciousness (what it is like). It addresses the easy problem, not the hard problem. Close to functionalism; not nondual in the strong sense.

6.3 Higher-order theories — Rosenthal, Lau
Consciousness is a representation of a representation — a system is conscious of a state when it has a higher-order representation of that state.

Honest status: rigorous; predicts some empirical patterns; but it pushes the hard problem one level up — what makes the higher-order representation conscious? Regress risk. Closer to functionalism than to nondual.

6.4 Attention Schema Theory — Graziano (2013)
The brain constructs a simplified model of its own attention; consciousness is the brain's model of its own attention. The model is a useful simplification, not a faithful representation.

Honest status: connects to the previous layer (attention); empirically productive; but it is a deflationary theory — consciousness is a model, not a fundamental feature. Closer to illusionism than to nondual.

6.5 Predictive processing / active inference — Friston, Clark
The brain is a prediction machine; perception is controlled hallucination; consciousness is what high-precision prediction feels like from the inside.

Honest status: unifying framework for much of cognitive neuroscience; rigorous (free-energy principle is mathematical); but the link to consciousness is suggestive, not derived. Close to functionalism; potentially nondual if interpreted as "prediction and experience are two descriptions of one process."

6.6 Analytic idealism — Kastrup (2018-)
Consciousness is the only fundamental; the physical world is the "extrinsic appearance" of mental states; individual minds are "dissociated alters" of mind-at-large.

Honest status: rigorous philosophical framework; consistent with much of physics (re-interprets it); but does not produce novel falsifiable predictions. A modern Advaita — consciousness fundamental, intelligence derivative. Closer to idealism than to strict nondual.

6.7 Hoffman's conscious realism — Hoffman (2008-)
Consciousness is fundamental; the physical world is a species-specific user interface; physical objects are icons, not realities.

Honest status:

Mathematically rigorous (the "interface theory of perception" has formal models)
Produces predictions (evolution favors fitness, not truth; perception should systematically misrepresent)
Multimodal user interface (MUI) theorem proven
But the consciousness-as-fundamental claim is not yet operationalized into testable predictions about consciousness itself
